{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested on exact head 80b921ea4522bbf6ba77fde9076ae7edf25ca2b4, primary docs/policy-pack.md, resting on all ten supplied artifacts.\n\nThe pack provides eleven named helper-built rules, the starter TypeScript/JSON pair, a plain-English replacement guide, policy-level carry cases, prospective reservation checks and pinned budget measurements. Root typecheck and all 180 candidate tests pass independently on both Node and workerd. The corrected workspace-relative compile invocation reproduces the committed policy.json byte for byte. The measured 500-path land/reservation case stays within the existing aggregate budget; the CPU estimate is properly identified as an estimate.\n\n1. P1 — The bundled starter creates unsatisfiable owner-review obligations for ordinary repository files (src/pack.ts ownerReview/starterPolicy; examples/demo-repo/.artroom/policy.ts and policy.json). ownerReview applies to [\"**\"], while the bundled owners map covers only src/api/**, src/** and docs/**. For README.md, package.json, tests/login.test.ts, wrangler.jsonc and .artroom/policy.json, the real runtime produces owners:[{path,owners:[]}] and a count-1 owner-review obligation whose sole from entry is \"owners\". My independent reproduction confirms these exact results on Node and workerd. Under R-OBL-2 no reviewer can qualify for that obligation, so an ordinary change on any of these paths cannot land; an additional CI/admin obligation does not satisfy this separate owner obligation. The unowned configuration paths also defeat the advertised normal admin-reviewed policy-edit flow. Give the demo complete, intentional owner coverage and make starterPolicy's owner-review coverage consistent with the supplied owner map. Require or configure a fallback owner, deliberately scope the owner rule to owned paths, or otherwise detect and explain unreviewable coverage; choose and document the intended behavior. Do not weaken Room admission or skip an open obligation. Cover root files, tests, deploy configuration, .artroom files, partially owned mixed proposals and custom partial owner maps, showing who can actually qualify rather than only asserting obligation IDs.\n\n2. P2 — The documented compile command fails from the repository root (docs/policy-pack.md Use it, the policy.ts header and scripts/compile-policy.ts directory resolution). Running the exact advertised command, npm run compile-policy --workspace @generalbusiness/artroom-policy -- examples/demo-repo/.artroom, returns ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND for packages/policy/examples/demo-repo/.artroom/policy.ts. npm executes the workspace script in packages/policy, and the compiler resolves arguments against that directory. The same invocation with ../../examples/demo-repo/.artroom succeeds and leaves the committed JSON unchanged. Make the documented command, compiler path convention and README agree, and verify the exact public command from its stated working directory. Keep the validated compiler.\n\nArchitecture conclusion: the pack belongs in the existing policy package, uses the shared helpers/runtime and adds no competing authority model. Keeping outside-claim, secret scanning, mandatory admin approval, obligation completion and clean-merge enforcement as platform rules is correct. The requested full policy-level scope is present; fix the starter's coverage so it can support actual day-one changes.\n\nSecurity conclusion: the expressions stay within the pinned profile, sensitive-prefix literals are safely encoded, inherited global inputs remain mandatory, and budget errors remain deterministic. The blocking issue is availability and policy maintainability: the shipped owner map makes routine and policy-file changes unreviewable without configuration-recovery. An admin obligation must remain additional protection rather than an accidental substitute for owners.\n\nSimplification conclusion: retain the eleven rules and the direct guide mapping to hooks, branch protection, CODEOWNERS and CI. Make ownership coverage explicit and the compile command directly runnable. No estimates or scheduling limits narrow the functionality. The isolated checker reproduction is /tmp/artroom-checker-pack/packages/policy/test/checker-pack.test.ts; it asserts the defect, not successful acceptance.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e959c45c38c9db0c63b6695ec122c1ca02660d6c","binding":"assigned","head":"80b921ea4522bbf6ba77fde9076ae7edf25ca2b4","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:904ab0f6547c92cfb5575f362560b8999c2f367e\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:7b400136cb5186a0418be52941fd4ab1994c4adf\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:904ab0f6547c92cfb5575f362560b8999c2f367e","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}